C-P3-r2-1 [Nit]. product-picture.tsx:25 gives each non-portrait picture the hint
'(min-width: 1024px) 25vw, 100vw'. Phase 3 moved the hero and the Reedy picture onto it.
The hero column is lg:col-span-6 (page.tsx:192, picture :194), about 44 to 45 percent of the viewport at
1024 to 1280 px. The Reedy column is lg:col-span-5 (page.tsx:289, picture :291), about 34 to 37 percent.
The phase base set 50vw (hero) and 40vw (Reedy) (git show 81df01cf2, page.tsx lines 182 and 286).
At lg and wider the browser picks a candidate about half as wide as the slot, so both pictures can look soft.
Fix: an optional sizes prop on ProductPicture, 50vw for the hero and 40vw for Reedy.
Code reading and layout arithmetic only, not a browser. These lines did not change in the remediation.

C-P3-r2-2 [Nit]. tutor-commission-calculator.tsx:434-436 shows graph.legCommissionBar ('Commission of each
leg') beside result.legPayouts, the sum of all legs. At preset 2 it reads 8,010 while each leg row shows
4,005 (:399). The adversarial test pins the sum (:350, :363). The results card names the same sum
'Leg commissions' (:214). Owner: the PR copy owner and the spec owner. Option: label the total with
results.legCommissions, or use legCommissionBar for the per-leg figure.

C-P3-r2-3 [Nit]. page.placeholders.test.tsx:76 checks only that a value exists. A page call with
{ n: '1' } passes the test, but each leg row would read 'Leg 1' because the component finds no '{n}'
(tutor-commission-calculator.tsx:393). Rule 11 asks for the literal { n: '{n}' }. Add an assertion that the
value for calculator.graph.legBar equals { n: '{n}' }. Code reading only, no mutation run.

